# Real financial documents. jorden-w/budget is public — a committed statement
# would be permanently in the git history, including after deletion.
#
# NOTE the trailing /* rather than a bare `statements/`: excluding the directory
# itself stops git descending into it, and the negations below would then be
# unable to re-include anything. Excluding the CONTENTS keeps them working.
statements/*
!statements/README.md

# merchant-rules.json stays local too. It's only merchant names, but they're
# the specific shops, bars and barbers you actually use, and on a public repo
# under your own name that's a map of where you spend your time. The skill
# re-learns rules cheaply; git history never forgets.

# Any budget-*.json holds real money data — transactions, card balances, pay,
# bankroll. Deliberately a wildcard rather than a list of known prefixes:
# listing them missed budget-setup-*.json and it came within one commit of a
# public repo. Wrongly ignoring a file is an annoyance; wrongly committing one
# is permanent. The wildcard is the safe side of that trade.
budget-*.json

# Local scratch
*.local.json
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
